From b265911c43b5f71e527f4ab01b23fc275c45af51 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Turnstone <turnstone@agents.invalid>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 17:38:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Turnstone: give the use button back (the regression Sandpiper
 found in my auto-eat work)

Sandpiper's second read found that my eating hold makes the bot look like a person
at the keyboard: LookBehavior#thePersonIsAtTheKeyboard answers partly on
options.keyUse.isDown(), eating holds exactly that key for a bite, and
Unattended#glanceIsOurs latches personIsHere on that answer and releases it only
when the next job takes control. So one idle meal silenced the bot's idle look and
head sway until something started a path. Confirmed in the source and fixed.

InputOverrideHandler: a releaseTheUseButton() that gives the button back only if
it was ours; an out-of-world tick now releases instead of returning early (their
observation about the Type.OUT early return was right - the key could be left down
out of world, and my javadoc had claimed a holder that stops ticking could not, so
that claim is corrected in place rather than quietly); and a narrow accessor
isUseButtonHeldForUs(), which is what a reader needs to tell our hold from a
person's press.

LookBehavior: the use button counts as a person arriving only when we are not
holding it. That is Sandpiper's proposed predicate exactly as proposed, and the
comment says so.

No census assertion was weakened: the file was already on the key-write list and
both shape pins still hold, which is why the fix went behind the existing surface
rather than past it. 425 tests, 0 failures.

Not claimed: the in-game check. Source-verified and suite-green is where this
stands; Sandpiper's scenario (idle, hungry, bread in hand, watch the head and the
latch for a minute) is what closes it and is still owed.
